<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potsdam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Potsdam is the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> just south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. A residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">the Great Elector</a> onwards, it became a garrison town under Frederick William I, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> built the palace of Sanssouci there. Its palaces and parks are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and in 1945 it hosted the Potsdam Conference.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potsdam.jpg" alt="Potsdam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kai Vogel · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Havel-Park-Lake-Babelsberg-Downtown-Potsdam-Green.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Frederick William I and Frederick the Great the Mark was the heartland of the kingdom, with Berlin and <b>Potsdam</b> as its seats of government and garrisons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After an imperial intervention, Japan told the Allies it would accept the <b>Potsdam</b> terms provided the Emperor's prerogatives were kept.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On the Glienicke Bridge between West Berlin and <b>Potsdam</b>, the US pilot shot down in 1960 was swapped for the KGB colonel Rudolf Abel.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
